validate/blocks_dev.jl — design phase of the haplotype-block lineage representation (src/block_lineages.jl)
Running log. Each step's hypotheses are written here BEFORE the step is run; the output is pasted
below them unchanged, then a short reading.

Disclosure. Development uses only inputs already seen by the linked-selection chain: the spectrum
diagnosis's D (13) and Dctl (8) inputs (validate/spectrum_diagnosis_inputs.csv), their 10 raw kernel
trajectories per input and their 40 E6′ kernel runs. D was chosen as the inputs on which :spectrum's
threshold call is wrong, so D is the hardest set for :spectrum by construction. The D, F, G, H′ and J
sets of validate/linked_decay.jl, linked_source.jl, linked_ld.jl and linked_dir.jl, the E6′ pool, the
spectrum_ld H pool and held-out set have all been seen, and are excluded from the fresh validation
(validate/blocks.jl). Nothing here is fitted to kernel outcomes: the block model has no free
parameter except the number of blocks B (and the partition rule), which is set by convergence in B
(B against 2B), not by agreement with the kernel.

== Candidates considered ==

(A) Haplotype-block lineages. Each chromosome is reduced to the sums y_b of its B blocks of
    consecutive loci. The life cycle is the kernel's, on N individuals. KEY OBSERVATION (exact, no
    closure): if nothing recombines inside a block, the block's sum is a sufficient state for it —
    a mutation anywhere in the block adds its effect to the sum, and the phenotype reads only sums.
    So "within-block allele structure" needs no model at all once crossovers are moved to block
    boundaries: the block model is EXACTLY the kernel with a coarsened crossover map, and with
    B = L it is the kernel itself (in law). The only approximation is the map. The map follows
    QTSim's lowest-point rule exactly (QTLSim.qtsim_switch_law: P(c) = Σ_K Pois(K; u)
    C(M−c−1, K−1)/C(M, K), M = L − 1), and a point inside a block is moved to the block's
    nearer boundary on the map. Partition :map puts the boundaries at equal steps of the map, so
    every block carries ≈ 1/B of the switches; :position uses equal numbers of loci.
    The suggested alternative, a within-block law for the recombinant block (e.g. a Gaussian or
    beta segregation model), needs exactly the within-block genic variance and LD that the
    linked-selection chain could not close; splitting the block in two is the exact version of it,
    i.e. B → 2B. So the within-block model is replaced by convergence in B.
(B) Hybrid (:spectrum per-site frequencies + an explicit stochastic population of block
    backgrounds feeding the association term). The explicit block population of (B) already
    yields V_G, the lag and W̄ — everything the emulator's chain needs — so the spectrum would add
    only a per-locus genic variance, at the price of a coupling closure (how the background
    association is shared among sites of different effects and frequencies), which is the part
    validate/linked_dir.log found cannot be closed at low order. (B) costs at least (A) plus
    :spectrum. Decision rule (stated now): build (B) only if (A) fails to converge at an
    affordable B or misses the kernel on the development inputs.
(C) Deterministic block moments. A Gaussian (second-order) block-moment closure is what
    `spectrum = (; ld = :dynamic)` already is (LD classes by position bin × split probability);
    the association needs fourth-order moments whose sources are high-order cumulants that cancel
    (linked_dir), and it is seeded by drift (genic_decline: 4N cuts it 5–15×), so a deterministic
    hierarchy must carry drift-generated moments to high order. Decision rule: not built unless
    (A) and (B) are weak; ld = :dynamic is reported as its second-order representative.

Criteria for the pick (development): (i) V_G through +200 of the move against the kernel;
(ii) the block-level association where measurable; (iii) survival to +2000 near the threshold
(threshold accuracy on D + Dctl against the 40 E6′ runs); (iv) cost per emulator call.

== Step 0: implementation check, B = L ==

Hypothesis (written before running). With one block per locus the block model is the kernel in
law. On the three 100-locus development inputs (06159, 09247, 07602), 40 block runs at B = 100
against the kernel's 40 E6′ runs and 10 trajectories: survival fractions not different (Fisher
p > 0.01 on each), V_G before the move within ±10% (10 kernel trajectories: SE of a mean ≈ 3–5%).
A failure here is a bug, not a modelling result.

