Reading. H1a, H1b and H1c are all REJECTED, decisively:
- V_G rises steadily with B and has not converged at B = 320: before the move the median change
  160 → 320 is 3.5% (:map) / 2.6% (:position), at +100 8.3% / 5.0%, at +200 16% / 12%; at 40 → 80
  it is 17% before the move and 31% at +100. At 1000 and 5000 loci V_G at B = 40 is 0.5–0.8 of its
  value at B = 320 (e.g. 12000: 0.0897 vs 0.1372 before the move; 0.0148 vs 0.0876 at +100).
- The survival calls barely move (most D inputs die at every B; the three 100-locus inputs flip
  from dead to alive between B = 20 and 80), so P is not a sensitive convergence measure here.
- :position converges somewhat FASTER than :map (H1c wrong): QTSim's map puts so few switches at
  the end of the chromosome that the :map partition's last blocks hold 10–20% of the loci, and the
  pairs inside them, although tightly linked (split probability ~1e-3–1e-2), are not unlinked
  enough to be treated as one non-recombining unit.
Why: removing recombination inside a block is not a small error even when the block's share of
the map is small. What matters is the split probability ρ of a pair relative to the drift and
selection time scales, 1/(2N) = 5e-4 and V_G/S (the δ of validate/genic_decline.md); a block of
map share 0.95/B leaves its pairs at ρ = 0 instead of up to 0.95/B, and with B ≤ 320 that is still
≫ 1/(2N). Non-recombining blocks then suffer Hill–Robertson interference (Hill & Robertson 1966;
Barton 1995) of their own: linked selection and drift inside a block reduce the variance it can
hold (and its response under the move). Convergence would need a block's map share ≪ 1/(2N),
i.e. B ≳ 2N = 2000, which is the kernel's own cost at these L. The exact block model (A) as built
(non-recombining blocks) therefore FAILS the convergence rule at every affordable B.

Decision (made now, before any comparison with the kernel at B < L): keep the representation, but
restore recombination inside blocks with a within-block law, the variant the task suggested:
(A2) Gaussian segregation within blocks. A crossover inside block b at a point that leaves a
fraction f of the block's loci on the starting strand gives the recombinant block
    y = ȳ_b + f (y_A − ȳ_b) + (1 − f)(y_B − ȳ_b) + √(2 f (1 − f) V_b) ξ,   ξ ~ N(0, 1),
where V_b is the variance of y_b among the parents' chromosomes. This is exact in law if the two
parts of the block are Gaussian, in linkage equilibrium with each other, and carry variance in
proportion to their numbers of loci: E[left | y] = f y and Var(left | y) = f(1 − f) V_b. It keeps
every crossover where the kernel puts it (no snapping), so a pair inside a block is split at its
kernel rate in the sense that the parts' association is broken; what it loses is the structure
inside the block (non-Gaussian allele distributions, LD within a part). It is a closure, so it can
be wrong; it is tested by convergence in B (B → 2B must change little: the closure then acts on
small pieces) and only then against the kernel.

== Step 1b: convergence in B with Gaussian segregation within blocks (A2) ==

A smoke test while implementing (one input, 00854, 6 runs, V_G before the move; not a test):
snap B 20 0.0042, gaussian B 20 0.0046, snap B 80 0.0069, gaussian B 80 0.0075 (snap B 320 in
step 1: 0.0079). So the within-block law recovers only part of the deficit at a given B.
Hypotheses (written before running). B ∈ {10, 20, 40, 80, 160}, both partitions, the same 21
inputs and 40 runs. H1b′: the Gaussian law converges faster in B than snapping (smaller
|log ratio| at each doubling), but, given the smoke test, the convergence rule of step 1 is
expected to be met at B = 160 at best, not at B ≤ 80. With one block per locus the two laws are
the same model (every switch is on a boundary).

Step 1c (added while step 1b ran; hypothesis written before running): the Gaussian law at B = 320
(the 18 inputs with L ≥ 500, same tag "gmap", seeds as step 1b's) changes V_G by less than 5% from
160 → 320 before the move and at +100 (i.e. it converges by 320, where snapping does not).

