Reading. V_G approaches the kernel monotonically in B for both laws: Gaussian B = 160 at 0.95, 0.96,
0.92, 0.93 of the kernel's (before, +50, +100, +200), B = 320 at 0.97, 0.97, 0.98, 0.97; snapping is
worse during the move at every B (B = 160: +200 at 0.78). The remaining bias at B = 160 is ~5–8%
low, i.e. the convergence rule leaves an error of that size; it is the same sign at every B (more
blocks → more variance), so the rule's 160 → 320 change underestimates the distance to B = L by
roughly a factor 2. Threshold calls agree with the kernel on all D + Dctl inputs at B ≥ 40 for
both laws, where the default :spectrum agrees on 8 of 21 (0 of the 13 D inputs, which were chosen
as :spectrum's failures). Caveat: most of these inputs die in the kernel (f = 0) far from the
threshold of the block model, so the calls are not a sensitive test; the fresh validation is.

== Choice (made here, before any fresh data) ==

Representation (A2): haplotype-block lineages, partition :map, Gaussian segregation within blocks,
B = 160 blocks (min(160, L); at L = 100 every locus is a block and the model is the kernel in
law), chosen by the convergence rule stated before step 1. Not (A) with snapping (no convergence
at B ≤ 320). (B) and (C) not built: (A2) converges at an affordable B on the development inputs and
reproduces the kernel's V_G trajectory within 5–8%, and the decision rules stated at the top
build (B)/(C) only if (A) fails. The cost is reported in step 4: it is NOT much cheaper than the
kernel at this B (see there), which is the main weakness of the representation.

