Reading. Hypothesis partly wrong: B = 160 with the Gaussian law (B = 100 at 100 loci) costs per call
of 40 replicates 28 s, 30 s, 20 s, 19 s at 100/500/1000/5000 loci against about 36, 40, 29, 54 s
for 40 kernel runs (×10 the 4-run time; these inputs die early, so all runs are short): 1.3×,
1.3×, 1.5×, 2.9× cheaper than the kernel, not 2×; and 4–9× slower than one deterministic
:spectrum call (2.3–6.1 s) or ld = :dynamic (4.0–6.1 s). The Gaussian law costs 1.3–1.6× snapping
at the same B (the per-block variance each generation). So the representation is NOT much cheaper
than the kernel: the kernel's own block genome (√L shared blocks, copy on write) is already
efficient, and a converged block model needs ~160 values per chromosome. This is recorded as the
main negative result of the design phase; the fresh validation tests accuracy, and (S4) cost.

